"(PDF) Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics of Scale (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392725253_Everyday_Climates_Household_Archaeologies_and_the_Politics_of_Scale)\nciteturn18view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392725253_Everyday_Climates_Household_Archaeologies_and_the_Politics_of_Scale\",\"lineno\":375}); Total lines: 2844\nL73: Academic Editors: Sharon Steadman\nL74: \nL75: and John Frederick Haldon\nL76: \nL77: Received: 15 April 2025\nL78: \nL79: Revised: 30 May 2025\nL80: \nL81: Accepted: 10 June 2025\nL82: \nL83: Published: 14 June 2025\nL84: \nL85: Citation: Kearns, C. Everyday\nL86: \nL87: Climates: Household Archaeologies\nL88: \nL89: and the Politics of Scale. Heritage 2025,\nL90: \nL91: 8, 227. https://doi.org/10.3390/\nL92: \nL93: heritage8060227\nL94: \nL95: Copyright: © 2025 by the author.\nL96: \nL97: Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.\nL98: \nL99: This article is an open access article\nL100: \nL101: distributed under the terms and\nL102: \nL103: conditions of the Creative Commons\nL104: Attribution (CC BY) license\nL105: \nL106: (https://creativecommons.org/\nL107: \nL108: licenses/by/4.0/).\nL109: \nL110: Article\nL111: \nL112: Everyday Climates: Household Archaeologies and the Politics\nL113: \nL114: of Scale\nL115: \nL116: Catherine Kearns\nL117: \nL118: Department of Classics, University of Chicago, Chicago, IL 60637, USA; cmkearns@uchicago.edu\nL119: \nL120: Abstract: The small scale is recognized as a necessary rebuttal to macroscalar narratives\nL121: \nL122: of climate–society relationships in the past, and archeologists and historians have increas-\nL123: ingly turned to advocating smaller and shorter scales of analysis and interpretation, from\nL124: \nL125: “microclimates” to interannual droughts and single settlement histories. Such provoca-\nL126: \nL127: tions rightly caution against the dangers of oversimpliﬁcation and determinism in recent\nL128: \nL129: planetary or Earth-systems approaches to human history, as well as push scholars to ac-\nL130: \nL131: knowledge human-scale experiences: weather, seasonality, landscape change. When it\nL132: comes to smaller-scale remains, however, like those of household practices, we often con-\nL133: \nL134: sider them data or proxies that validate larger-scale arguments about societal persistence\nL135: \nL136: or economic vulnerability. Y et the material and ideational ways that people in premod-\nL137: \nL138: ern worlds made sense of their surroundings, especially via gendered and class-based\nL139: \nL140: rhythms of production and consumption, were deeply entwined in the politics of everyday\nL141: household life. What would a household archeology of climate entail? In this paper I\nL142: \nL143: highlight how households themselves were critical sites of environmental construction,\nL144: \nL145: experience and history-making through a selection of examples of archeological work\nL146: \nL147: from the Mediterranean. I argue that archeologists can critically rethink themes of persis-\nL148: \nL149: tence and adaptation by taking seriously the scalar constructions and varied politics of\nL150: \nL151: domestic life.\nL152: Keywords: scale; household archeology; Mediterranean; everyday; climate\nL153: \nL154: 1. Introduction\nL155: \nL156: Climate change feels big. W e recognize, to begin, that climate is a complex process\nL157: \nL158: whose interlocking dynamics and variables of atmospheric, oceanic, terrestrial and biotic\nL159: \nL160: components operate at large scales. While we often acknowledge that humans primarily\nL161: \nL162: experience climatic changes through weather, by perceiving transitions in expected norms\nL163: and intimately engaging with shifting conditions of temperature or precipitation, we\nL164: \nL165: naturally turn to the bigger, regional and even global idea of climate to make sense of\nL166: \nL167: things [\nL168: \nL169: 1\nL170: \nL171: ,\nL172: \nL173: 2\nL174: \nL175: ]. When we then move to study premodern environments, that big scale, both\nL176: \nL177: temporal and spatial, seems ﬁtting and even necessary, given the often coarse and widely\nL178: \nL179: varying records we have to reconstruct and interpr et past environments. As archeologists,\nL180: we speak most conﬁdently in terms of societal relationships to climate, through structural\nL181: \nL182: forces like subsistence economies, institutions, migration practices, settlements or cultural\nL183: \nL184: ideas of nature. We tend to seek out diachronic patterns and r egional variations in evidence\nL185: \nL186: that can provide comparative information on the impacts of climatic or environmental\nL187: \nL188: changes and how human societies might have responded differently across time and space.\nL189: A big-scale approach to premodern environmental or climatic history has therefor e\nL190: \nL191: stuck, as historians and archeologists have turned to examining ancient human–climate\nL192: \nL193: Heritage 2025,8, 227 https://doi.org/10.3390/heritage8060227\nL194: \nL195: Heritage 2025,8, 227 2 of 20\nL196: \nL197: interactions and to the recognition that environmental records ar e key to more robust\nL198: \nL199: interpretations of historical transformation [\nL200: \nL201: 3\nL202: \nL203: ,\nL204: \nL205: 4\nL206: \nL207: ]. Shifts in historiographical conversations\nL208: lend support to this larger focus—globalizing, deep or even planetary history has gained\nL209: \nL210: standing for its ability to synchronize varying complicated sets of data to narrate diachronic\nL211: \nL212: processes of societal growth and decline, topics clearly, even obviously, relevant today [\nL213: \nL214: 5\nL215: \nL216: ].\nL217: \nL218: In new ﬁelds like the “history of climate and society,” for example, scholars advocate analy-\nL219: \nL220: ses that integrate multiple kinds of natural proxies and historical and material evidence to\nL221: reveal the complexities of past responses to and interventions in environmental changes [\nL222: \nL223: 6\nL224: \nL225: ].\nL226: \nL227: The uneven resolution of many natural records, as well as the challenges of seeking out\nL228: \nL229: signiﬁcant patterns from complex background noise, often attenuate our focus to centuries-\nL230: \nL231: long or millennia-long transformations. Despite these issues, these studies are clearly\nL232: \nL233: attractive for their apparent complexity in analyzing coupled human–nature systems [\nL234: \nL235: 7\nL236: \nL237: ,\nL238: \nL239: 8\nL240: \nL241: ].\nL242: In that vein, systems theory is re-emerging in archeological studies of societal persistence\nL243: \nL244: and adaptation, as recent work in resilience and sustainability studies shows [\nL245: \nL246: 9\nL247: \nL248: –\nL249: \nL250: 11\nL251: \nL252: ]. Schol-\nL253: \nL254: ars acknowledge the risks of thinking big in recent planetary or Earth-systems approaches\nL255: \nL256: to human history—reductionism and overgeneralization, even determinism—but advocate\nL257: \nL258: the heuristic power of these frameworks, based on the records we have, e.g., [\nL259: \nL260: 4\nL261: \nL262: ]. Climate\nL263: change in the past, then, can still feel big.\nL264: \nL265: Efforts to descend beneath graphs of global atmospheric concentrations of carbon or\nL266: \nL267: regional surveys of data to think about people’s real, lived or even imagined experiences\nL268: \nL269: with climatic or environmental change would seem, then, important to complementing this\nL270: \nL271: macroscalar analytical bent ([\nL272: \nL273: 12\nL274: \nL275: ,\nL276: \nL277: 13\nL278: \nL279: ], see also [\nL280: \nL281: 2\nL282: \nL283: ]). Archeologists have certainly called out the\nL284: poor resolution of some proxies, as opposed to records such as tr ee rings or speleothems,\nL285: \nL286: as well as the lack of people in many big environmental histories, the multiple and often in-\nL287: \nL288: commensurate scales of practice that forged human–climate relationships and the nuanced,\nL289: \nL290: nonlinear realities of the impacts of droughts, ﬂoods or other phenomena on premodern\nL291: \nL292: life [\nL293: \nL294: 13\nL295: \nL296: –\nL297: \nL298: 15\nL299: \nL300: ]. For the ancient Mediterranean, for example, we are increasingly more aware\nL301: of modeling the effects of acute droughts of 2–3 years on rainfed agriculture, as opposed\nL302: \nL303: to the potential longer-term impacts of regional trends in aridity [\nL304: \nL305: 16\nL306: \nL307: ]. Often, attempts to\nL308: \nL309: integrate multiscalar investigations of human–environment relationships circle around the\nL310: \nL311: measure of social hierarchies and macropolitical change—ruling elites or state institutions,\nL312: \nL313: on one side, and masses of populations on the other [\nL314: \nL315: 17\nL316: \nL317: ,\nL318: \nL319: 18\nL320: \nL321: ]. It is more challenging, based\nL322: on the available evidence and methods, to reach other scales of environmental and climatic\nL323: \nL324: histories: villages, communities, household networks and families [19].\nL325: \nL326: Y et the household was the center of life in premodern and preindustrial sedentary soci-\nL327: \nL328: eties throughout the Mediterranean, the regional focus of this paper. Indeed, up to the onset\nL329: \nL330: of industrialization, the household unit—typically deﬁned as a kin-based, co-residential\nL331: collective but not necessarily a nuclear family, given that their construction was historically\nL332: \nL333: and culturally contingent—was a crucially important site of production, consumption\nL334: \nL335: and the management of energy ﬂows between people and larger collectives [\nL336: \nL337: 20\nL338: \nL339: ,\nL340: \nL341: 21\nL342: \nL343: ]. It is\nL344: \nL345: for this reason, for example, that archeologists turn to long-term comparative analyses\nL346: \nL347: of household size to study phenomena like inequality from the past to the present [\nL348: \nL349: 22\nL350: \nL351: ].\nL352: Beyond resource or wealth accumulation, where people lived and worked conditioned their\nL353: \nL354: understandings, perceptions and imaginations of landscapes, environments and senses of\nL355: \nL356: climate [\nL357: \nL358: 12\nL359: \nL360: ,\nL361: \nL362: 23\nL363: \nL364: ]. Even with Western industrialization during the 18th and 19th centuries\nL365: \nL366: CE, as recent work is showing, relationships between energy consumption and climate\nL367: \nL368: change can be traced to domestic needs around coal and the choices and decisions that\nL369: changed daily life, directed economic strategies and redesigned the home [\nL370: \nL371: 24\nL372: \nL373: ]. It seems\nL374: \nL375: important to forward an archeology of the household-scale practices that mediated peo-\nL376: \nL377: ple’s experiences of shifting conditions through their norms, ideas and decisions relating\nL378: \nL379: Heritage 2025,8, 227 3 of 20\nL380: \nL381: to resources, landscapes, weather and climate. In other words, to think critically about\nL382: \nL383: how past people “domesticated” their environments, by examining how they established\nL384: ideas and expectations of climate through everyday activities and understandings of social\nL385: \nL386: orders operating at shorter rhythms and place-based scales [\nL387: \nL388: 1\nL389: \nL390: ,\nL391: \nL392: 25\nL393: \nL394: ,\nL395: \nL396: 26\nL397: \nL398: ]. I argue this not to\nL399: \nL400: rebalance our macroscalar investigations by prioritizing only the microscale as a way to\nL401: \nL402: explain historical change, but to advocate the study of mundane and quotidian climates\nL403: \nL404: through the tempos and spatialities of domestic life. In the Mediterranean, households and\nL405: dwelling structures comprise most of the spatial area of the sites we investigate, even if\nL406: \nL407: they remain woefully unexcavated in pursuit of larger, public or state-controlled structures\nL408: \nL409: in dense urban environments. Household archaeologies can thus offer important evidence\nL410: \nL411: of norms of subsistence or logics of production, particularly of women and others often\nL412: \nL413: elided from history.\nL414: \nL415: This paper acts as a provocation to center ancient households as the point of inquiry.\nL416: I begin by highlighting issues in the current ﬁeld of (pre)historical climate studies and\nL417: \nL418: the importance of incorporating data and interpretive frameworks of smaller and shorter\nL419: \nL420: spatial and temporal scales. Multiscalar analyses are key to highlighting the diversity\nL421: \nL422: and complexity of human perceptions and experiences of environments and changing\nL423: \nL424: ecologies forged through everyday practice. They also expose the generalizations of the\nL425: functionalist work that treats households as data points in larger arguments about land\nL426: \nL427: use or urbanization. I then turn to recent scholarship in the archeology of households\nL428: \nL429: and highlight insights on ﬂows of materials, energy and labor: storage, consumption,\nL430: \nL431: foodways and gendered production. Examples from archeological contexts reveal how\nL432: \nL433: everyday practices conditioned how people experienced shifting environments and built\nL434: up senses and norms of climate. Despite the inherent challenges of studying microscalar\nL435: \nL436: or daily practices in the ancient world, as well as the difﬁculties extending or connecting\nL437: \nL438: embodied, individual choices to a speciﬁc climatic change, I end with some concluding\nL439: \nL440: thoughts on how household archaeologies can contribute to pressing conversations on\nL441: \nL442: climate and sustainability. As I have argued elsewhere [\nL443: \nL444: 27\nL445: \nL446: ], I take climate to refer to the\nL447: mediations between people and their perceptions and experiences, through bodily and\nL448: \nL449: material encounters, of weather, ecological conditions and natural phenomena. Households,\nL450: \nL451: then, should open us up to thinking of climate alongside and in dialog with statistics of\nL452: \nL453: regional meteorological data.\nL454: \nL455: Instead of aiming for an exhaustive coverage of one or two case studies, I sample from\nL456: \nL457: a range of recent scholarship to articulate broader lines of inquiry and to provoke further\nL458: discussions. I focus on the ancient Mediterranean for a few reasons (Figure 1). First, there\nL459: \nL460: is a wealth of data from differentiated household contexts as well as, for many historical\nL461: \nL462: periods, textual sources that can help contextualize and compare patterns of domestic\nL463: \nL464: activity [\nL465: \nL466: 28\nL467: \nL468: ]. Household studies have increasingly exposed the heterogeneity of ancient\nL469: \nL470: domestic built environments, reminding us that we cannot assume the functionality or\nL471: makeup of households nor the transhistorical concepts of public/private. Second, advances\nL472: \nL473: in paleoenvironmental and paleoclimatic research have made the ancient Mediterranean\nL474: \nL475: a particularly robust place to explore premodern human–environment r elationships. As\nL476: \nL477: a result, there is also a growing literature on ancient climate change in Mediterranean\nL478: \nL479: archeology and ancient history and available data from which to draw insights (e.g., [\nL480: \nL481: 29\nL482: \nL483: –\nL484: \nL485: 31\nL486: ]). And ﬁnally, the varied environmental and climatic conditions of Mediterranean\nL487: \nL488: regions, with millennia-long histories of resource use and landscape manipulation, offer\nL489: \nL490: compelling contexts in which to explore the relationality between households and changing\nL491: \nL492: ecosystems [32].\nL493: \nL494: Heritage 2025,8, 227 4 of 20\nL495: \nL496: \nL497: \nL498: Figure 1. Sites mentioned in text. Map created by author.\nL499: \nL500: 2. Going Smaller\nL501: \nL502: The inﬂux of more data and more highly resolved natural archives, like speleothems,\nL503: sediment cores and tree rings, has ramped up scholarly (and public) interest in past\nL504: \nL505: climates and environments. Consequently, the ﬁeld has turned to self-r eﬂexively consider\nL506: \nL507: its methodological and interpretive frameworks ([\nL508: \nL509: 14\nL510: \nL511: ,\nL512: \nL513: 33\nL514: \nL515: ] and other articles in this issue).\nL516: \nL517: Among these is the issue of scale, both analytical and of past practices. The Anthropocene,\nL518: \nL519: once proposed as a geological epoch meant to capture humanity’s geophysical capacity to\nL520: \nL521: intervene in Earth systems [\nL522: \nL523: 34\nL524: ] but recently voted against by the International Commission\nL525: \nL526: on Stratigraphy Subcommission on Quaternary Stratigraphy [\nL527: \nL528: 35\nL529: \nL530: ,\nL531: \nL532: 36\nL533: \nL534: ], hovers over much of\nL535: \nL536: this discourse. If humanity, as a species, is now capable of manipulating and transforming\nL537: \nL538: the Earth’s atmosphere and surfaces, we need interpretive tools that think along global\nL539: \nL540: and planetary lines. Archeologists have rightly critiqued the conceits and presumptions\nL541: \nL542: of the Anthropocene [\nL543: \nL544: 37\nL545: \nL546: –\nL547: \nL548: 39\nL549: ], but the turn to considering how past societies responded to\nL550: \nL551: or altered Earth systems has similarly privileged large-scale analyses. Take for example\nL552: \nL553: “collapse” studies of imperial decline due to aridiﬁcation and demographic crises through\nL554: \nL555: the study of agricultural economic instability and “mega-droughts” [18,40–42].\nL556: \nL557: As noted above, the wide aperture we use to analyze societal responses or adap-\nL558: \nL559: tations to changing climates emerges in large part from the evidence we have at hand.\nL560: In the Mediterranean, regions with abundant sources of accessible paleoenvironmental\nL561: \nL562: data, like caves, freshwater lakes and standing forests, have provided starting points for\nL563: \nL564: reconstructing past environments through pr oxies of temperature and moisture changes,\nL565: \nL566: but these proxies come with diverse methodological issues for chronological precision,\nL567: \nL568: and many still require expensive radiocarbon dating and statistical methods to improve\nL569: \nL570: their resolution [\nL571: \nL572: 43\nL573: ]. These records might produce trends in oscillations in local or regional\nL574: \nL575: temperatures at annual, decadal or centennial scales. For other regions, there exist no or\nL576: \nL577: very few robust paleoenvironmental studies, forcing scholars to rely on interr egional data\nL578: \nL579: or hemispheric records, like arctic ice cores [\nL580: \nL581: 44\nL582: \nL583: ]. The dependency on non-local histories of\nL584: \nL585: ecological or climatic change thus also contributes to macroscalar spatial generalizations\nL586: based on correlation and synchronization rather than direct evidence of causation. In\nL587: \nL588: addition, the use of survey evidence to chart changing regional settlement patterns, while\nL589: \nL590: capable of revealing diachronic trends in r esponses to broader landscape changes, typically\nL591: \nL592: Heritage 2025,8, 227 5 of 20\nL593: \nL594: relies on surface collections without systematic stratigraphic testing, further coarsening\nL595: \nL596: interpretations.\nL597: \nL598: More recent applications of systems theory in archeology, in the form of resilience and\nL599: sustainability studies, attempt to overcome these issues by studying societal interactions\nL600: \nL601: with triggering climatic and environmental events or processes through examinations of\nL602: \nL603: persistence and disruption, often at landscape scales ([\nL604: \nL605: 45\nL606: \nL607: –\nL608: \nL609: 49\nL610: \nL611: ]; see also [\nL612: \nL613: 9\nL614: \nL615: –\nL616: \nL617: 11\nL618: \nL619: ]). Indeed,\nL620: \nL621: in a recent bibliometric synthesis of publications using resilience and/or sustainability\nL622: \nL623: concepts or frameworks, Jacobson [\nL624: \nL625: 11\nL626: \nL627: ] found two papers out of dozens that analyzed\nL628: resilient practices at the scale of the household, in distinction from regional communities,\nL629: \nL630: states or empires. Most studies of resilient societal adaptations to climate change are\nL631: \nL632: quantitative more than qualitative, using numbers of households or sites as proxy evidence\nL633: \nL634: for the stability and coherence of state or institutional structures, like food supply [\nL635: \nL636: 29\nL637: \nL638: ].\nL639: \nL640: Envisioning externalized forces like climate change primarily as impacts on society, scholars\nL641: can then measure whether human groups or sociopolitical structures wer e “more” or “less”\nL642: \nL643: resilient to stresses. These studies can often remain essentializing by inferring or assuming\nL644: \nL645: pre-existing social conditions that determine who is vulnerable and who is not along coarse\nL646: \nL647: models of political or economic status. Resilience discourse can also mystify the causes or\nL648: \nL649: material habits of vulnerability, masking over differentiated social responses or encounters\nL650: with environmental change, and can downplay the structural determinants of those changes\nL651: \nL652: in favor of reactionary outcomes [\nL653: \nL654: 50\nL655: \nL656: ,\nL657: \nL658: 51\nL659: \nL660: ]. In addition, relying on systems frameworks or\nL661: \nL662: larger analytical scales can obscure how societal well-being was created primarily through\nL663: \nL664: the smaller-scale, everyday household practices of resource accumulation. As Bowes [\nL665: \nL666: 52\nL667: \nL668: ],\nL669: \nL670: p. 32 has argued, “We need to keep the urgency to tell big stories, but use thick, small-scale\nL671: data to tell them, returning to a place we actually know quite well—the household—and\nL672: \nL673: exploiting both well-known and new datasets in the context of lives lived—not trends\nL674: \nL675: already assumed.”\nL676: \nL677: Archeologists and historians have increasingly turned to advocating for smaller and\nL678: \nL679: shorter scales of analysis and the interpretation of integrated scalar relationships, given\nL680: \nL681: that looping, incremental or gradual changes are the mediations through which past people\nL682: experienced and made sense of climate [53–55]. But as with paleoenvironmental data, the\nL683: \nL684: evidence available for Mediterranean households can make those moves tricky, especially\nL685: \nL686: in regions that lack the kinds of robust records of excavate d or surveyed households and\nL687: \nL688: smaller social units. The majority of our evidence comes from urban residential areas, with\nL689: \nL690: fewer rural or non-urban domestic residences available to study [\nL691: \nL692: 56\nL693: \nL694: ]. While household\nL695: or domestic archaeologies have been on the rise over the last few decades, providing\nL696: \nL697: variable and cross-cultural records, even within the Mediterranean there remain ar eas\nL698: \nL699: without commensurate data with which to compare household sizes, material histories and\nL700: \nL701: associated assemblages. Furthermore, the intersections between household studies and\nL702: \nL703: environmental and climatic histories are only beginning to gain attention. Conceptions of\nL704: households and the “home” are often embedded in modern, bourgeois juxtapositions of\nL705: \nL706: the outside world of nature with the interior, private spaces of social life, conditioning us\nL707: \nL708: to keep environmental histories outside the domestic [57].\nL709: \nL710: And yet as argued above, households were a key venue for the conjoining of human–\nL711: \nL712: environment interactions through lived experiences, “vital sites for the production and\nL713: \nL714: reproduction of nature\nL715: \nL716: . . .\nL717: \nL718: they also articulate with technologies of power while engaging\nL719: in ﬂows of matter, ener gy, capital, and knowledge” [\nL720: \nL721: 58\nL722: \nL723: ], p. 174. In past Mediterranean\nL724: \nL725: societies based on agricultural economies, signiﬁcant amounts of production and consump-\nL726: \nL727: tion happened within households and estates of varying sizes, and people built their social\nL728: \nL729: worlds through dwelling and working with their families and local kinship networks,\nL730: \nL731: with diversely constructed ties to other scales of communities, states and regions [\nL732: \nL733: 51\nL734: \nL735: ].\nL736: \nL737: Heritage 2025,8, 227 6 of 20\nL738: Households were co-constituted through the micropolitics of daily life and the macrolevel\nL739: \nL740: structures and decision-making of larger forces, like tribes, ethnic formations or states\nL741: \nL742: and empires. Studying domestic realms of production, storage, consumption and social\nL743: \nL744: networks can therefore reveal important patterns of lived experience from bottom-up\nL745: \nL746: perspectives that privilege interconnections between people and their environments, not\nL747: \nL748: just external or exogenous triggers or shocks to the system [\nL749: 59\nL750: \nL751: –\nL752: \nL753: 61\nL754: \nL755: ]. Such frameworks can\nL756: \nL757: center social actors themselves, rather than abstract entities that adapt or react as parts of\nL758: \nL759: larger sub-systems. While archaeologies of individuals and their embodied experiences\nL760: \nL761: remain incredibly difﬁcult to interpret, households of fer a way to study the lives of under-\nL762: \nL763: represented people, especially those who existed outside our available textual records [\nL764: \nL765: 62\nL766: \nL767: ].\nL768: \nL769: Through household material assemblages and the remains of built environments, we can\nL770: start to identify decision-making that shaped and was shaped by environments, as well as\nL771: \nL772: how these efforts and practices changed over time ([63]; see also [21]). In other words, we\nL773: \nL774: can investigate how households were not just passive and self-contained spaces but were\nL775: \nL776: instrumental to the making of nature [64].\nL777: \nL778: But what is a household? The ﬁeld of archeology has worked over the last few decades,\nL779: \nL780: especially since the provocation to study domestic contexts by Wilk and Rathje [\nL781: \nL782: 65\nL783: \nL784: ], to\nL785: reﬁne archeological and conceptual deﬁnitions borne from highly diverse, heterogeneous\nL786: \nL787: material records [\nL788: \nL789: 66\nL790: \nL791: ]. We can think of households as gr oupings of co-resident people,\nL792: \nL793: and their nonhuman networks, engaged in meaningful relationships and similar tasks,\nL794: \nL795: acting together socioeconomically [\nL796: \nL797: 67\nL798: \nL799: ,\nL800: \nL801: 68\nL802: \nL803: ]. Or, we might call them archeologically identiﬁ-\nL804: \nL805: able through physical structures that combine interior and exterior spaces for tasks and\nL806: \nL807: dwelling [\nL808: \nL809: 65\nL810: ]. Importantly, households do not equate universally to the nuclear family,\nL811: \nL812: given the wide-ranging evidence for the co-residence of multiple constructed groups, and\nL813: \nL814: extend beyond the walls of built structures to include spaces like compounds and court-\nL815: \nL816: yards, refuse dumps and work areas like gardens, ﬁelds, pens and thr eshing ﬂoors. But\nL817: \nL818: equally important are self-reﬂexive attempts to interrogate relations of people, as a unit\nL819: called a household, and the material residues of their actions in what we deﬁne as archeo-\nL820: \nL821: logical contexts of households. Scholars have used household records to create comparative\nL822: \nL823: analyses across space and time or to follow material evidence to examine thematic issues,\nL824: \nL825: such as domestic scales of craft and food production, consumption and even identity [\nL826: \nL827: 69\nL828: \nL829: –\nL830: \nL831: 71\nL832: \nL833: ]. Others confront households as a cultural trait, social historical practice or category of\nL834: built environment in speciﬁc periods and regions ([\nL835: \nL836: 72\nL837: \nL838: ], see also [\nL839: \nL840: 28\nL841: \nL842: ]). For many, households\nL843: \nL844: are important indices of socioeconomic change due to a presumed conservatism in their\nL845: \nL846: material constraints and resistance to innovation [\nL847: \nL848: 73\nL849: \nL850: ]. As the ﬁeld of household archeology\nL851: \nL852: has developed, the attention to the ecological contexts of household activities has also\nL853: \nL854: grown, particularly through the study of micromorphology, discard practices of botanical\nL855: and faunal remains and resource management or capture, for example, in the form of water\nL856: \nL857: storage [74].\nL858: \nL859: When considered conceptually as social groups whose activities mediated relation-\nL860: \nL861: ships between families, communities and social structures, households are additionally\nL862: \nL863: important for grounding studies of everyday and daily life [\nL864: \nL865: 55\nL866: \nL867: ,\nL868: \nL869: 75\nL870: \nL871: ]. Building on the ideas\nL872: \nL873: of practice theory as well as geographies of domesticity and micropolitics, archeologists\nL874: have turned to household scales to explore the routines, habits and decisions that become\nL875: \nL876: interrelated with the social production of space [\nL877: \nL878: 76\nL879: \nL880: –\nL881: \nL882: 78\nL883: \nL884: ]. A focus on everyday tempos can\nL885: \nL886: further connect to experiences of seasonality and weather, which wer e critical to devel-\nL887: \nL888: oping norms and values associated with understandings of climates and their effects on\nL889: \nL890: local or regional surroundings [\nL891: \nL892: 79\nL893: \nL894: ]. It is important to remain attentive, however, to how\nL895: the quotidian rhythms of household life were not separated from larger pr ocesses and\nL896: \nL897: structures, nor determined by ecological or climatic concerns. As Foxhall reminds [\nL898: \nL899: 21\nL900: \nL901: ], p.\nL902: \nL903: Heritage 2025,8, 227 7 of 20\nL904: \nL905: 417, households were dialectically intertwined with these forces at varying constructed\nL906: \nL907: scales, which together shaped homes and landscapes through the demands of practical\nL908: \nL909: needs, cultural ideals, as well as more coordinated and regulated expectations of political\nL910: formations, through institutions like property and inheritance or taxation and tribute: “to\nL911: \nL912: what extent were household decisions shaped by constraints emanating from, on the one\nL913: \nL914: hand, other members of the communities in which they lived and worked, and on the\nL915: \nL916: other hand by political and economic forces extending beyond the local community?” (see\nL917: \nL918: also [\nL919: \nL920: 80\nL921: \nL922: ]). These interconnections between the household and society make it critical to\nL923: situate studies of domestic climates against historically contingent forms of power and\nL924: \n"
